                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide who to keep, develop or exit. <strong>Conceria San Frediano</strong> supplies <strong>22% of certified leather</strong> and is one of only two chrome-free, animal-welfare-certified tanneries — six weeks from default. Letting it fail leaves you single-sourced on certified leather. Then bring the fragile <strong>~€200k artisanal workshops</strong> into the programme — a standard bank platform they cannot onboard to excludes the very tier you needed to protect. And <em>finance</em> the certification upgrades through sustainability-linked discounts rather than merely <em>demanding</em> them by the audit date.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Integrate, stress-test & defend the plan`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Assemble the SCF instrument, supplier decisions and sustainability criteria into one programme and stress-test it against three clocks: the CFO's <strong>€30M target</strong>, the client <strong>audit</strong>, and San Frediano's <strong>6-week</strong> liquidity clock. Does the plan actually free €30M? Does it keep certified leather alive? Does it pass the client code? The facilitator role-plays the CFO (cash), San Frediano's owner (survival) and the audit lead (compliance). The trap: pushing DPO harder for the last few million and breaking the chain — or declaring victory and leaving no one to govern it next quarter.`,

                    r1_lever_label:         `The CFO wants €30M freed fast. What is your working-capital posture?`,
                    r1_segment_label:       `How do you segment the supply base before acting?`,
                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `How clearly does your team name the trap (cheap cash lever = €190M revenue risk)? (%)`,
                    r1_diagnosis_readout:   `Clarity that freeing cash must not break the chain:`,
                    lever_scf:              `Free cash via supply-chain finance — extend buyer DPO while a financier pays suppliers early (positive-sum)`,
                    lever_negotiate:        `Stretch DPO selectively, only with the large mills that can absorb it; protect the fragile tier`,
                    lever_stretch:          `Stretch DPO to 120 days across all 220 suppliers — it hits €30M the cheapest way`,
                    lever_inventory:        `Ignore payables — cut inventory and chase receivables instead`,
                    seg_kraljic:            `By criticality, financial fragility and substitutability — flag the critical-but-fragile tier to develop`,
                    seg_spend:              `By spend only — squeeze the biggest line items first`,
                    seg_none:               `No segmentation — apply one uniform rule to all 220 suppliers`,

                    r2_instrument_label:    `Which supply-chain-finance instrument anchors the programme?`,
                    r2_target_label:        `Effective DPO you set with the financier (75 baseline; 120 = blunt stretch)`,
                    r2_dpo_hint:            `Days. With reverse factoring, extending YOUR DPO frees cash while suppliers are still paid early by the bank. Beyond ~105 days the cash gain thins and SME strain rises.`,
                    r2_rate_label:          `How do you set the financing rate offered to suppliers?`,
                    r2_fund_label:          `ESG / supplier-finance fund you commit now (€M, cap €12M)`,
                    r2_fund_hint:           `€M to subsidise onboarding, certification upgrades and sustainability-linked discounts. Too little and the fragile tier and the ESG cascade stall.`,
                    inst_reverse:           `Reverse factoring / approved-payables finance — bank pays suppliers early at your AA rating, you extend DPO (frees cash + helps suppliers)`,
                    inst_dynamic:           `Dynamic discounting — use your own cash for early-payment discounts (helps margin, spends cash)`,
