                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_scf:           `<strong>Good instinct.</strong> You're reaching for a financing instrument rather than a blunt payment stretch — the only route that can free cash and keep the fragile tier alive at the same time. Now prove it with the right instrument and a segmentation that protects the critical workshops.`,
                    coach_r1_negotiate:     `<strong>Selective stretching is safer than a blanket one</strong> — leaning on the mills that can absorb it spares the fragile tier. But on its own it leaves most of the €30M on the table. A supply-chain-finance instrument can free far more cash without the strain.`,
                    coach_r1_stretch:       `<strong>You freed cash by paying everyone 45 days later.</strong> The CFO is delighted — for six weeks. Then San Frediano defaults, you lose 22% of your certified leather, the client audit fails, and <em>€190M of contracts is at risk</em>. You solved a €30M cash problem by creating a €190M revenue problem. Was there a way to free the cash without breaking the chain?`,
                    coach_r1_inventory:     `<strong>Inventory and receivables matter, but they're not the bind.</strong> The CFO's lever is payables and the trap is what stretching them does to the fragile tier. Don't look away from the supplier-finance question — that's where the €30M and the €190M collide.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> A financing instrument <em>and</em> a Kraljic-style segmentation that flags the critical-but-fragile tier to develop — that is exactly the lens that lets you free cash without detonating the supply base. Now design the instrument.`,
                    coach_r1_spend:         `<strong>Segmenting by spend alone misses the point.</strong> The biggest line items are the large mills that can cope; the danger sits in the small, critical, fragile workshops. Segment by criticality and fragility, not just by how much you buy.`,
                    coach_r1_noseg:         `<strong>One uniform rule for 220 suppliers is how you break the chain.</strong> A rule that's fine for a large mill is fatal for a €200k artisanal workshop. Segment first — criticality, fragility, substitutability — or you'll squeeze exactly the tier you needed to protect.`,
                    coach_r1_blind:         `<strong>You're not naming the trap.</strong> If the team can't articulate that the cheapest cash lever puts €190M of revenue at risk, it will reach for the DPO stretch under pressure. Name the bind clearly before you act.`,
                    coach_r2_reverse:       `<strong>Reverse factoring is the positive-sum move.</strong> A bank pays your suppliers early at your AA rating while you extend your own DPO — you free cash <em>and</em> suppliers get paid faster, cheaper. The art now is how far to push DPO and how to reach the suppliers who can't self-onboard.`,
                    coach_r2_dynamic:       `<strong>Generous — and exactly backwards.</strong> You're paying suppliers early with your own cash for a discount. Margin improves, but the CFO asked you to <em>free</em> €30M and you just <em>spent</em> cash to do the opposite. How do you help suppliers <em>and</em> free cash at the same time? That's reverse factoring.`,
                    coach_r2_factoring:     `<strong>Classic factoring pushes the cost onto suppliers.</strong> They sell their receivables at their own weaker rates — costlier for exactly the fragile tier you're trying to protect, and it frees you less cash than an approved-payables programme at your rating.`,
                    coach_r2_none:          `<strong>Direct renegotiation is the DPO stretch in a quieter suit.</strong> Without a financier in the middle, every extra day of your DPO is a day of cash drained from suppliers who can't fund it. You free some cash and strain the chain doing it.`,
                    coach_r2_anchor:        `<strong>This is the anchor of the whole programme.</strong> Reverse factoring frees your cash and pays suppliers early, and a sustainability-linked rate turns financing into the lever that drives the ESG upgrades your clients demand. Finance and sustainability in one instrument.`,
                    coach_r2_tiered:        `<strong>Reverse factoring is right; the rate design is a missed lever.</strong> Pricing purely on credit risk gives the best rates to the suppliers who least need help. Tie the rate to verified ESG performance and you finance the certification cascade at the same time.`,
                    coach_r3_rescue:        `<strong>You saved the tannery.</strong> Fast early payment through the SCF programme plus a certification-upgrade plan keeps 22% of your certified leather alive and gives you redundancy instead of a single source. That is resilience bought cheaply relative to €190M.`,
                    coach_r3_qualify:       `<strong>You let San Frediano fail and are scrambling to qualify the other tannery.</strong> Now you're 100% dependent on a single certified-leather source, with no leverage and zero redundancy on 22% of your leather. One fire, one strike, one price hike and you're exposed. Did “replaceable” mean “safe to lose”?`,
                    coach_r3_exit:          `<strong>You decided San Frediano is replaceable and exited it.</strong> That accepts single-source concentration on certified leather — the textbook way to turn a procurement saving into a strategic vulnerability. Resilience just took a structural hit you can't easily undo.`,
                    coach_r3_reach:         `<strong>You reached the tier the programme is meant to protect.</strong> Concierge onboarding — simplified paperwork, field support, a platform adapted to micro-suppliers — brings the €200k workshops in. An SCF programme that the fragile tier can actually use is the whole point.`,
                    coach_r3_standard:      `<strong>The standard bank platform quietly excludes the fragile tier.</strong> The €200k artisanal workshops lack the paperwork and can't onboard the way a large mill does. The exact tier you needed to protect is the one your programme leaves out. How do you bring them in?`,
                    coach_r3_exclude:       `<strong>You set up the programme at your AA rating — clever — and excluded the fragile tier.</strong> The small workshops can't onboard, lack the paperwork, and aren't covered. You freed your cash and left the irreplaceable artisanal base outside the very mechanism designed to save it.`,
                    coach_r3_mandate:       `<strong>You've told the supply base to be chrome-free, certified and living-wage compliant by the audit.</strong> The big mills can; the small workshops can't fund €200k of upgrades on your terms. So they fail the code, you drop them, and your “Made in Italy” artisanal base collapses — the provenance your clients are paying for. <em>Finance</em> compliance, don't just demand it.`,
                    coach_r3_starve:        `<strong>You've starved the fragile tier of the fund.</strong> Steering almost nothing to the critical-but-fragile workshops means the financing reaches the suppliers who least need it. The fund exists to develop the tier that secures €190M — point it there.`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>An integrated programme is what survives the stress test.</strong> The SCF frees the cash, suppliers stay paid early, certification is financed, and you can show the full reconciliation. That's a plan that holds against all three clocks at once.`,
                    coach_r4_cashfirst:     `<strong>You pushed DPO harder to close the last few million.</strong> The CFO smiles, but you've drained more cash from suppliers who can't fund it — strain rises and the chain frays for marginal cash. Closing the gap on the backs of the fragile tier is how a €30M win becomes a €190M loss.`,
                    coach_r4_suppliersfirst:`<strong>Protecting everyone is admirable, but you missed the €30M.</strong> Asking the CFO for more time may be honest, yet the mandate was real and the deadline is the same quarter as the audit. The point of reverse factoring was that you didn't have to choose — you could free the cash <em>and</em> protect suppliers.`,
                    coach_r4_positivesum:   `<strong>That's a board-ready, positive-sum case.</strong> €30M freed, San Frediano alive, the codes financed, and the trade-offs named honestly. The CFO gets the cash, the owner survives, the auditor passes the code — the rare answer where all three clocks are satisfied.`,
                    coach_r4_cfoonly:       `<strong>You led on cash and buried the fragility.</strong> The CFO is happy today, but the supplier and compliance risk you downplayed is exactly what detonates next quarter. A pitch that only satisfies the cash clock fails the other two.`,
                    coach_r4_optimistic:    `<strong>Declaring it solved is how the chain breaks quietly.</strong> Assuming suppliers and the audit “fall into line” is the optimism that turns a €30M win into a missed audit and a lost tannery. Stress-test the plan against reality, not against hope.`,
                    coach_r4_treasury:      `<strong>Treasury watching the cash number isn't governance.</strong> If no one owns the supplier scorecards and the sustainability-linked rate review, the programme decays the moment the crisis quarter ends. Name an owner across finance and sourcing.`,
                    coach_r4_none:          `<strong>No standing owner is how this unravels.</strong> The crisis passes, the team redeploys, the rates never get reviewed, and the fragile tier slips back toward distress. A supply-chain-finance programme is a standing capability, not a one-quarter rescue.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
