                    sum_r2_weak:            `Wrong instrument or rate for the goal.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Rescued the tannery, reached the tier, financed compliance.`,
                    sum_r3_break:           `Exited critical supply or mandated without financing.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Suppliers and ESG only partly protected.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Integrated, positive-sum, governed plan.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Cash-only pitch or no standing governance.`,

                    insight_positivesum:    `Positive-sum programme: you freed the €30M, kept San Frediano and the fragile tier alive, and financed the sustainability cascade — turning a zero-sum cash grab into a solution all three stakeholders can sign. This is reverse factoring plus sustainability-linked SCF used exactly as intended.`,
                    insight_grab:           `Cash grab: you hit the €30M, but by draining suppliers who can't fund it. Supplier resilience collapsed and the chain is breaking — the cheapest working-capital lever became the most expensive revenue decision.`,
                    insight_exposed:        `You hit the cash target but left suppliers exposed. The CFO is satisfied this quarter, yet a fragile, under-financed supply base puts the €190M of contracts and the brand's provenance at risk the next.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of the €30M target. Refusing the destructive stretch was right, but without the right instrument — reverse factoring frees cash without breaking the chain — you left the CFO's mandate unmet.`,
                    insight_sanfrediano:    `You lost Conceria San Frediano: 22% of certified leather now runs through a single source, with no redundancy and no leverage. Resilience is capped — one disruption away from a supply crisis on your most sensitive material.`,
                    insight_codes:          `Sustainability readiness stayed low — the supply base is unlikely to pass the client codes. Mandating compliance the artisanal tier can't fund, or pricing financing without an ESG link, leaves the cascade unfinanced.`,
                    insight_dpo:            `You pushed effective DPO to the danger zone. Even with a financier in the middle, terms this long strain the fragile tier — the cash you freed is borrowed against supplier survival.`,
                    insight_cascade:        `You used financing as a sustainability lever: tying preferential rates to verified ESG performance drove the certification upgrades the small workshops could never have funded alone. Liquidity, cost and compliance aligned in a single instrument.`,

                    hint_grab:              `You're freeing cash by stretching payments onto suppliers who can't fund it. Anchor the programme on reverse factoring so a financier — not the fragile tier — carries the early payment.`,
                    hint_sanfrediano:       `You've lost or are about to lose San Frediano and your certified leather is single-sourced. Rescue and develop the critical tannery; redundancy on 22% of your leather is cheap relative to €190M.`,
                    hint_resilience:        `Supplier resilience is dangerously low. Reach the fragile tier with concierge onboarding, keep the critical tannery, and steer the fund to the critical-but-fragile workshops.`,
                    hint_sustain:           `Sustainability readiness is low — the supply base won't pass the client codes. Finance the upgrades through sustainability-linked rates instead of mandating compliance suppliers can't fund.`,
                    hint_cash:              `You're well short of the €30M. Reverse factoring lets you extend your own DPO to free cash while the bank keeps suppliers paid early — push the instrument, not the blunt stretch.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — cash freed, resilience and sustainability healthy. Now lock in a named owner and an annual rate review so the programme survives the crisis quarter.`,

